<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Effectiveness Across Model Families</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We found that our training recipe, which includes a data mix of traditional safety data and the new compliance dataset trained with SFT plus GRPO, extends well to many model families. To produce the values in the table, for each size model in each family (limited to sizes 1B-8B as available), we record the delta between the base model and our finetuned model. The reported value is the average of these deltas across the model sizes in the family.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A1.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Change in F1 score after using Compliance training recipe compared with base model. Scores averaged across all 1B-8B model sizes available in the model family.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A1.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Family</span></td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1 Increase WildGuard</span></td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1 Increase Compliance</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Qwen3</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">+28.4</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">+22.5</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Qwen2.5</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+13.8</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+36.0</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T4.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T4.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Llama3.2</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">+35.4</td>
<td id="A1.T4.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">+21.3</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Ablations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we ablate the reasoning component of the DynaGuard models and separately ablate the inclusion of synthetic Compliance data in the training pipeline, in order to determine how much of the performance gain was due to reasoning ability, and how much was due to training on Compliance Data. We can see that there is a significant lift from just training on the Compliance training set, which shows that our generation of diverse policies is a valuable component, but that training for reasoning also further improves performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
